Kavat: the system hides and shows the parts of a browser window around the page, live.

The parts are the tab strip, the toolbar with the address bar, the bookmarks bar and the
side panel; zen mode is the four of them hidden, the page alone, in a window that stays
tiled: it is not fullscreen. An extension cannot reach them, so the browser watches a file
the system writes, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/kavat-browser/parts, with the names of the parts that
are hidden: "tabs", "toolbar", "bookmarks", "sidepanel", or "zen" for the four, separated
by spaces or lines. A file that is empty or missing hides nothing. It is read away from the
UI thread, with base::FilePathWatcher on a sequence that may block, as the upgrade
detector's DirectoryMonitor does, and what it says is applied on the UI thread to every
normal window of the browser, open or opened later.

Only the visibility predicates of BrowserView change: ShouldDrawTabStrip, IsToolbarVisible
and IsBookmarkBarVisible say false for a part that is hidden, the way fullscreen already
takes the tab strip and the toolbar away, and the window lays itself out again as it does
on leaving fullscreen. The side panel is closed when it becomes hidden. App windows,
popups, devtools and picture-in-picture are not normal windows and keep their own parts;
fullscreen works as before. Window features are left alone: the commands that read them,
like a new tab, would otherwise open new windows.

With the toolbar hidden there is no address bar, and the extension's button, which the
console opens from, is hidden with it. Ctrl+L shows the toolbar again, until the file
next changes, so a window with only its page always has a way out.

